      video: "https://www.pw.live/watch/?type=penpencilvdo&mode=public-feeds&cameFrom=test-series&testId=69f4e5ea8180c1555cab2da8&questionId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea78e&batchId=&testCategoryId=&parentId=6960b6755c7508c1bb165ed9&childId=69f4e5ea8180c1555cab2da8&vType=TEST_SERIES_QUESTION&secondaryChildId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea78e&secondaryParentId=undefined&src=https%3A%2F%2Fd1d34p8vz63oiq.cloudfront.net%2F8ccde396-aeb4-4c9b-ad3c-177ac10ba3c8%2Fmaster.mpd&is_comments_enabled=false&is_report_enabled=true&is_rating_enabled=true&is_products_enabled=false&is_header_enabled=true&typeKey=BATCH_QUIZ_VIDEO_SOLUTION&reportTypeKey=questionId&typeId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea78f&back_button=false",
      text: `<p>Consider the following statements:</p>
<p>S1: A graph has a unique Minimum Spanning Tree if and only if all its edge weights are distinct.</p>
<p>S2: An edge having the minimum weight in the graph is always included in every Minimum Spanning Tree.</p>
<p>Which one of the following option is correct?</p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
        `<p>Both S1 and S2 are true.</p>`,
        `<p>S1 is true and S2 is false.</p>`,
        `<p>S1 is false and S2 is true.</p>`,
        `<p>Both S1 and S2 are false.</p>`,
      ],
      answer: "D",
      solution: `<p><a href="https://www.pw.live/watch/?type=penpencilvdo&mode=public-feeds&cameFrom=test-series&testId=69f4e5ea8180c1555cab2da8&questionId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea78e&batchId=&testCategoryId=&parentId=6960b6755c7508c1bb165ed9&childId=69f4e5ea8180c1555cab2da8&vType=TEST_SERIES_QUESTION&secondaryChildId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea78e&secondaryParentId=undefined&src=https%3A%2F%2Fd1d34p8vz63oiq.cloudfront.net%2F8ccde396-aeb4-4c9b-ad3c-177ac10ba3c8%2Fmaster.mpd&is_comments_enabled=false&is_report_enabled=true&is_rating_enabled=true&is_products_enabled=false&is_header_enabled=true&typeKey=BATCH_QUIZ_VIDEO_SOLUTION&reportTypeKey=questionId&typeId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea78f&back_button=false" target="_blank" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;padding:8px 16px;background:#5a4bda;color:#ffffff;border-radius:6px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;margin:8px 0;">▶ Watch Video Solution (00:04:00)</a></p>
<p>Statement S1 is false. If all edge weights are distinct, then the graph has a unique MST. However, the converse is not true. A graph may have repeated edge weights and still possess a unique MST.</p>
<p>For example, consider the cycle graph&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> with edge weights 1, 1, and 2. Although the edge weights are not distinct, the unique MST consists of the two edges having weight 1. Hence, distinct edge weights are sufficient but not necessary for the uniqueness of the MST.</p>
<p>Statement S2 is also false. An edge with the minimum weight in the graph need not belong to every MST.</p>
<p>For example, consider the cycle graph&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> with all three edges having weight 1. Every spanning tree consists of any two of these three edges. Thus, each edge has the minimum weight, but any one of them can be omitted from an MST. Therefore, a minimum-weight edge is included in at least one MST, but not necessarily in every MST.</p>
<p>Hence, both S1 and S2 are false, and the correct answer is (D).</p>`
