      answer: 4,
      solution: `<p><a href="https://www.pw.live/watch/?type=penpencilvdo&mode=public-feeds&cameFrom=test-series&testId=69f4e5ea8180c1555cab2da8&questionId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea76e&batchId=&testCategoryId=&parentId=6960b6755c7508c1bb165ed9&childId=69f4e5ea8180c1555cab2da8&vType=TEST_SERIES_QUESTION&secondaryChildId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea76e&secondaryParentId=undefined&src=https%3A%2F%2Fd1d34p8vz63oiq.cloudfront.net%2Fe929aa75-89e4-4590-831d-1debb01d6adf%2Fmaster.mpd&is_comments_enabled=false&is_report_enabled=true&is_rating_enabled=true&is_products_enabled=false&is_header_enabled=true&typeKey=BATCH_QUIZ_VIDEO_SOLUTION&reportTypeKey=questionId&typeId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea76f&back_button=false" target="_blank" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;padding:8px 16px;background:#5a4bda;color:#ffffff;border-radius:6px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;margin:8px 0;">▶ Watch Video Solution (00:03:22)</a></p>
<p>Applying Kruskal's algorithm, the edge of weight 1 is always included. Both edges of weight 2 are also included since they do not form a cycle. The two edges of weight 3 create cycles with the already selected edges and are therefore discarded. Among the two edges of weight 4, exactly one must be selected, giving 2 choices. Similarly, among the two edges of weight 5, exactly one must be selected, giving another 2 choices.</p>
<p>Therefore, the total number of distinct Minimum Spanning Trees is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo></math></p>`
