      text: `<p>Consider the following algorithm for finding the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) of two strings&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>.</p>
<p>Step 1: Enumerate all possible subsequences of&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and store them in a set A.</p>
<p>Step 2: Enumerate all possible subsequences of&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and store them in a set B.</p>
<p>Step 3: Compute the intersection <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">A</mi><mo>&#8745;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi></math>.</p>
<p>Step 4: If <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">A</mi><mo>&#8745;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi></math> contains only empty string then return the empty string. Otherwise, return the longest string in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">A</mi><mo>&#8745;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi></math>.</p>
<p>If&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>"</mo><mi>ABCDEF</mi><mo>"</mo></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>"</mo><mi>ACBEFDG</mi><mo>"</mo><mo>,</mo></math></p>
<p>Determine the total number of subsequences generated during Steps 1 and 2.</p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
        ``,
        ``,
        ``,
        ``,
      ],
      answer: 192,
      solution: `<p><a href="https://www.pw.live/watch/?type=penpencilvdo&mode=public-feeds&cameFrom=test-series&testId=69f4e5ea8180c1555cab2da8&questionId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea7bb&batchId=&testCategoryId=&parentId=6960b6755c7508c1bb165ed9&childId=69f4e5ea8180c1555cab2da8&vType=TEST_SERIES_QUESTION&secondaryChildId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea7bb&secondaryParentId=undefined&src=https%3A%2F%2Fd1d34p8vz63oiq.cloudfront.net%2F29386060-b51e-4597-92ad-76112cb72b6b%2Fmaster.mpd&is_comments_enabled=false&is_report_enabled=true&is_rating_enabled=true&is_products_enabled=false&is_header_enabled=true&typeKey=BATCH_QUIZ_VIDEO_SOLUTION&reportTypeKey=questionId&typeId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea7bc&back_button=false" target="_blank" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;padding:8px 16px;background:#5a4bda;color:#ffffff;border-radius:6px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;margin:8px 0;">▶ Watch Video Solution (00:07:30)</a></p>
<p>Since the algorithm explicitly returns the empty string when no non-empty common subsequence exists, the empty string must also be generated while enumerating the subsequences.</p>
<p>A string of length n has <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math> subsequences, including the empty subsequence.</p>
<p>The length of&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> is 6.</p>
<p>Hence, the number of subsequences generated in Step 1 is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></math>.</p>
<p>The length of&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is 7.</p>
<p>Hence, the number of subsequences generated in Step 2 is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>7</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></math></p>
<p>Therefore, the total number of subsequences generated during the first two steps is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>64</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>128</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>192</mn><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p>Hence, the required answer is 192.</p>`
