      answer: "D",
      solution: `<p><a href="https://www.pw.live/watch/?type=penpencilvdo&mode=public-feeds&cameFrom=test-series&testId=69f4e5ea8180c1555cab2da8&questionId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea7ca&batchId=&testCategoryId=&parentId=6960b6755c7508c1bb165ed9&childId=69f4e5ea8180c1555cab2da8&vType=TEST_SERIES_QUESTION&secondaryChildId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea7ca&secondaryParentId=undefined&src=https%3A%2F%2Fd1d34p8vz63oiq.cloudfront.net%2F4da8ab78-66e5-4a57-80d1-38b334618385%2Fmaster.mpd&is_comments_enabled=false&is_report_enabled=true&is_rating_enabled=true&is_products_enabled=false&is_header_enabled=true&typeKey=BATCH_QUIZ_VIDEO_SOLUTION&reportTypeKey=questionId&typeId=6a842b5ca3d58651e9bea7cb&back_button=false" target="_blank" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;padding:8px 16px;background:#5a4bda;color:#ffffff;border-radius:6px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;margin:8px 0;">▶ Watch Video Solution (00:07:47)</a></p>
<p>Let <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></math>. Then the given functions become log k, k, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></msup></math>, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></msup></math>, and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math>,</p>
<p>Clearly,&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>
<p>Now compare the remaining three functions by taking logarithms. They become k log k, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>logn</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>logk</mi></math>, and klogk &lt; klogn, respectively. Since k <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>klogn</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nlogk</mi></math> for sufficiently large k, we obtain&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></msup><mo>&#60;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></msup><mo>&#60;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math></p>
<p>Substituting back, we get <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>logn</mi></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mi>logn</mi></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math>.</p>
<p>Therefore, the increasing order of the given functions is&nbsp;</p>
<p><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>logn</mi></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mi>logn</mi></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msup></math></p>
<p>Hence, the correct answer is (D).</p>`
