      video: "https://www.pw.live/watch/?type=penpencilvdo&mode=public-feeds&cameFrom=test-series&testId=69f4ee120897516718e866a5&questionId=6a969f22efd299bb977e969c&batchId=&testCategoryId=&parentId=6960b6755c7508c1bb165ed9&childId=69f4ee120897516718e866a5&vType=TEST_SERIES_QUESTION&secondaryChildId=6a969f22efd299bb977e969c&secondaryParentId=undefined&src=https%3A%2F%2Fd1d34p8vz63oiq.cloudfront.net%2F98f872dc-9010-4537-b9b6-06fc0684957c%2Fmaster.mpd&is_comments_enabled=false&is_report_enabled=true&is_rating_enabled=true&is_products_enabled=false&is_header_enabled=true&typeKey=BATCH_QUIZ_VIDEO_SOLUTION&reportTypeKey=questionId&typeId=6a969f22efd299bb977e969d&back_button=false",
      text: `<p style="text-align: justify;">A computer system employs a paged virtual memory architecture with address translation support through a Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB). The TLB is organized as a 2-way set associative, consisting of 128 sets, where each TLB entry stores the corresponding page translation information along with associated protection bits. It is observed that the processor can generate a maximum of 262144 distinct logical addresses without incurring any TLB miss. Given that the virtual memory system supports 4M pages <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>20</mn></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></math> the size of the logical address space supported by the system is _____ GBytes?</p>`,
      image: "",
      options: [
        ``,
        ``,
        ``,
        ``,
      ],
      answer: 4,
      solution: `<p><a href="https://www.pw.live/watch/?type=penpencilvdo&mode=public-feeds&cameFrom=test-series&testId=69f4ee120897516718e866a5&questionId=6a969f22efd299bb977e969c&batchId=&testCategoryId=&parentId=6960b6755c7508c1bb165ed9&childId=69f4ee120897516718e866a5&vType=TEST_SERIES_QUESTION&secondaryChildId=6a969f22efd299bb977e969c&secondaryParentId=undefined&src=https%3A%2F%2Fd1d34p8vz63oiq.cloudfront.net%2F98f872dc-9010-4537-b9b6-06fc0684957c%2Fmaster.mpd&is_comments_enabled=false&is_report_enabled=true&is_rating_enabled=true&is_products_enabled=false&is_header_enabled=true&typeKey=BATCH_QUIZ_VIDEO_SOLUTION&reportTypeKey=questionId&typeId=6a969f22efd299bb977e969d&back_button=false" target="_blank" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;padding:8px 16px;background:#5a4bda;color:#ffffff;border-radius:6px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;font-size:14px;margin:8px 0;">▶ Watch Video Solution (00:11:51)</a></p>
<p>Number of pages = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>22</mn></msup></math>, hence page number <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="normal">P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>22</mn></math> bits</p>
<p>Number of entries in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>TLB</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>*</mo><mn>128</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></math></p>
<p>Each TLB entry takes up to a process page. Which means a maximum of 256 pages can have TLB hit which means 256 frames can have 256 pages in main memory.</p>
<p>Assume each page size x bytes. Which means each page has x number of logical addresses.</p>
<p>Total number of TLB hit logical addresses = 262144</p>
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>256</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>*</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>page</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>size</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>262144</mn></math></p>
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi>Page</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>size</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></math></p>
<p>If page size <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1024</mn></math> then offset <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math> bits</p>
<p>Virtual address divided into 2 parts like:</p>
<p><img title="2.png" src="https://static.pw.live/5eb393ee95fab7468a79d189/QBG/f7916017-8fa3-46a9-9c82-f67172632681.png" alt="" width="165" height="51" /></p>
<p>Logical address = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>21</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>bits</mi></math></p>
<p>Logical address space <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>32</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>Gbytes</mi></math></p>`
